[Music]  welcome back to cbs mornings the fight  over texas new abortion law is just the  latest in a series of battles around roe  v wade for decades after the supreme  court upheld the right to an abortion  the efforts to challenge this  controversial decision well it's been  constant the woman at the center of the  case was known first as jane rowe but  her real name was norma mccorvey she was  a mother of two when she filed a case  and a mother of three by the time it was  decided  jan crawford spoke with mccorvey's  oldest daughter her name is melissa  mills they spoke at her home in texas  this is a candid conversation about a  very complicated history and this is her  first tv interview  not everybody's meant to be a mother and  i didn't expect that of her she was the  mother she could be as a young girl  melissa mills didn't know her mother  norma mccorvey was known to everyone  else as jane rowe a complicated at times  dysfunctional woman melissa was raised  by her maternal grandmother how would  you describe your mother  she was really funny she was the life of  the party  i didn't think of her as a mother figure  i thought of her more like a sister  because that's how our relationship was  melissa was almost a teenager when she  realized her mother was jane roe of roe  versus wade that's when she also learned  she had two younger half sisters  jennifer ferguson and shelley thornton  shelley was the child known as baby ro  journalist joshua prager spoke to the  three women in his upcoming book the  family row an american story proportions  shared with the atlantic i think most  people have no idea that there is a baby  row we were all given up for adoption  but my grandparents adopted me  and the other two were given up at birth  she couldn't take care of herself much  less anybody else the sisters met back  in 2013. we only had like 24 hours  together it was very short and we got as  much in as we could according to  prager's book thornton refused to meet  mccorvey the woman who told the world  she didn't want her shelley never met  her and i think it was just too much  and i understand that um when somebody i  mean she wanted to abort her and she  told shelley that  she expected her to thank her for not  doing so  mom had  mom had a sick sense of humor i mean she  was her humor was pretty harsh and she  was mad at shelley because shelley  wouldn't do a dna test when shelley  wouldn't it kind of you know  kind of made her mad made her mad as the  only daughter who knew mccorvey growing  up mills vowed she would be different  you wanted stability  and civility and family family yep how  are you able to do that  i guess when you want something you do  it  what are you most proud of  my kids  and the way you've been able to be the  kind of mother  that you didn't have right  mccorvey felt she never fit in used by  both sides at some point she  crossed over and  started speaking on the other side right  against abortion rights yeah she felt  guilty and people made her feel bad for  the part and the role that she played  with the roe versus wade case  and all the babies that were aborted  through the years and people would call  her a killer they called her satan they  called her all kinds of terrible things  it was it was cruel mccorvey spent the  last year of her life in texas near  melissa and melissa's two daughters she  died in 2017 leaving behind a legacy  that remains as divisive as ever you  know we all like feel protective of our  moms and they're mentally protected yeah  that's it seems like you i think that's  human nature right like you  even if your mom has flaws that's your  mom  what was that like for you  it was hard  but i didn't want anybody to hurt her  and i felt she was even though she was  strong she was fragile  she was strong but fragile  and you could see that  jan crawford reporting there uh it's a  powerful piece powerful interview the  family row is the name for it how  fitting in a way that the complicated  relationship we all have to abortion as  a country is contained within jane rowe  herself who has been on both sides of  the issue i mean it just goes to show  you there's family drama it seems in all  things i think most people don't even  realize that baby roe was actually born  i know she filed the case and then but  it took so long the baby came so it  became a point i think it's interesting  that shelley when you learn her name is  shelley didn't want to meet her mother  and when you think about what she been  through you're right and then saying you  know when you want something you just do  it do it um i know she grew up  conflicted given everything um that  happened to her as a child but now she's  like you know what i want to be a great  mother to my daughters yeah there's an  endless legacy and that's one of the  better parts of this legacy no doubt  we're going to be talking to the author  of that book by the way on monday who  actually sat down with all three of the  daughters including shelley i think  he'll have a lot to say i think so it's  a very big book  there's a lot to say a reporter and a  great writer and this is a different  kind of book for him so i'm looking  forward to it and jan thank you again  great interview

